<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of British Columbia (1866-1871)</h1><p class="st-meta">1866 CE – 1871 CE · capital New Westminster; later Victoria</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The united Pacific colony of Vancouver Island and the mainland, which joined Canada in 1871 in return for a transcontinental railway.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Debts left by the gold rushes and the cost of two administrations led <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> to merge the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-vancouver-island">Colony of Vancouver Island</a> with the mainland <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-british-columbia-1858-1866">Colony of British Columbia</a> (1858-1866) in November 1866, under Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-seymour">Frederick Seymour</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-westminster">New Westminster</a> was the first capital, but after bitter rivalry <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-victoria">Victoria</a> was chosen in 1868.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony was governed by a governor and a partly elected legislative council. Its economy was depressed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cariboo">Cariboo</a> gold declined, and its population of settlers was small next to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-nations-in-canada">First Nations</a> majority. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> Purchase of 1867, some residents favoured annexation to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>, while others, led by the journalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amor-de-cosmos">Amor De Cosmos</a>, campaigned to join <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/canada">Canada</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-musgrave">Anthony Musgrave</a>, appointed in 1869, steered the colony towards Confederation. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/canada">Canada</a> agreed to take over its debt and to begin a railway to the Pacific within two years, completing it within ten, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> became a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> on 20 July 1871.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1866 CE – 1871 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">North America and Oceania</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.84%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1866 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>New Westminster; later Victoria</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick Seymour, Anthony Musgrave</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Columbia (colony)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1868&amp;focus=colony-of-british-columbia-1866-1871" aria-label="Colony of British Columbia (1866-1871) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1846 462 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of British Columbia (1866-1871) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2166 718L2137 741M2166 718L2166 718M2166 718L4997 651M2166 718L3203 831"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2137 741h0M2166 718h0M4997 651h0M3203 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2166 718h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2166 718h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks New Westminster. <a href="/atlas?year=1868&amp;focus=colony-of-british-columbia-1866-1871">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
